1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,787
A customs broker has a new product to file.

2
00:00:02,787 --> 00:00:06,681
Somewhere among about nineteen thousand eight hundred tariff lines

3
00:00:06,681 --> 00:00:08,333
is the exact ten digit code.

4
00:00:08,333 --> 00:00:10,339
A wrong code means the wrong duty.

5
00:00:10,339 --> 00:00:12,581
I built this to help the broker find a

6
00:00:12,581 --> 00:00:14,646
code worth reviewing before filing.

7
00:00:15,756 --> 00:00:17,800
The user enters a product description.

8
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:20,866
My system searches earlier customs rulings and the tariff

9
00:00:20,866 --> 00:00:23,555
schedule, then a small model uses that evidence to

10
00:00:23,555 --> 00:00:25,868
choose a code. The user sees the answer and

11
00:00:25,868 --> 00:00:27,751
the schedule lines found by search.

12
00:00:27,751 --> 00:00:29,257
A person reviews the choice.

13
00:00:29,257 --> 00:00:30,548
Saving time is the goal,

14
00:00:30,548 --> 00:00:31,731
not a measured result.

15
00:00:32,891 --> 00:00:35,912
I built search over twenty seven thousand eight hundred

16
00:00:35,912 --> 00:00:38,548
forty one real customs rulings and the schedule,

17
00:00:38,548 --> 00:00:40,306
using both keywords and meaning.

18
00:00:40,306 --> 00:00:43,271
The rulings come from United States Customs and Border

19
00:00:43,271 --> 00:00:46,017
Protection. Search returns twenty candidate lines.

20
00:00:46,017 --> 00:00:48,050
I kept the agency's own descriptions,

21
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:51,015
removed answers, and used only government code labels.

22
00:00:51,015 --> 00:00:53,652
I fine tuned a four billion parameter model with

23
00:00:53,652 --> 00:00:56,947
small trainable adapters. It runs on one graphics processor.

24
00:00:57,828 --> 00:00:59,994
I enter a men's jacket from a real test

25
00:00:59,994 --> 00:01:02,105
ruling issued after all training data.

26
00:01:02,105 --> 00:01:04,105
It is ninety five percent polyester,

27
00:01:04,105 --> 00:01:05,826
five percent elastane, knitted,

28
00:01:05,826 --> 00:01:08,604
brushed inside, with a zip front and banded cuffs.

29
00:01:08,604 --> 00:01:11,325
I turn on the base comparison and click classify.

30
00:01:11,325 --> 00:01:13,603
Both models get the same search evidence.

31
00:01:13,603 --> 00:01:15,602
The fine tuned answer appears first,

32
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:16,769
in about two seconds,

33
00:01:16,769 --> 00:01:17,658
at the top left.

34
00:01:17,658 --> 00:01:20,435
Below it are the twenty tariff lines search found,

35
00:01:20,435 --> 00:01:22,046
with the chosen line in bold.

36
00:01:22,046 --> 00:01:24,656
The base model's card still says it is running,

37
00:01:24,656 --> 00:01:26,434
using that same set of evidence.

38
00:01:26,434 --> 00:01:29,155
While we wait, those construction details matter:

39
00:01:29,155 --> 00:01:30,433
this jacket is knitted,

40
00:01:30,433 --> 00:01:32,210
with no coated fabric described.

41
00:01:32,210 --> 00:01:34,210
The base takes about thirty seconds.

42
00:01:34,210 --> 00:01:35,376
Here are the results:

43
00:01:35,376 --> 00:01:38,154
the tuned model gets the knitted men's jacket line

44
00:01:38,154 --> 00:01:39,320
in about two seconds.

45
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:41,764
The base picks a coated fabric garment line,

46
00:01:41,764 --> 00:01:42,875
which is wrong here.

47
00:01:46,025 --> 00:01:48,329
I enter a second example I picked from the

48
00:01:48,329 --> 00:01:49,809
test set, not a random one,

49
00:01:49,809 --> 00:01:51,784
also issued after all training data.

50
00:01:51,784 --> 00:01:54,691
This is an empty lipstick container made of injection

51
00:01:54,691 --> 00:01:58,201
molded plastic. Its turning mechanism raises and lowers lipstick

52
00:01:58,201 --> 00:02:00,011
and protects it during transport,

53
00:02:00,011 --> 00:02:02,698
storage, and use. Both models get the same search

54
00:02:02,698 --> 00:02:04,014
evidence. While we wait,

55
00:02:04,014 --> 00:02:06,428
watch whether each answer fits the material.

56
00:02:06,428 --> 00:02:09,225
Below the answer cards are the twenty tariff search

57
00:02:09,225 --> 00:02:11,693
results. Look for the chosen line among them,

58
00:02:11,693 --> 00:02:14,654
then compare it with the product description while the

59
00:02:14,654 --> 00:02:16,190
base model is still running.

60
00:02:16,190 --> 00:02:19,042
My tuned model is right about sixty percent overall.

61
00:02:19,042 --> 00:02:21,894
Here, it picks the plastic packing and carrying line

62
00:02:21,894 --> 00:02:23,046
in under two seconds.

63
00:02:23,046 --> 00:02:24,527
After about thirty seconds,

64
00:02:24,527 --> 00:02:27,269
the base picks a glass perfume or toilet container

65
00:02:27,269 --> 00:02:29,353
line, despite the plastic description.

66
00:02:32,231 --> 00:02:33,960
On five hundred fresh rulings,

67
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:35,862
I scored exact ten digit matches.

68
00:02:35,862 --> 00:02:39,320
These rulings span August twenty twenty five through October

69
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:42,779
twenty twenty six. Search alone scored thirty eight percent.

70
00:02:42,779 --> 00:02:44,738
The base, Qwen three four billion,

71
00:02:44,738 --> 00:02:46,179
scored forty two percent.

72
00:02:46,179 --> 00:02:49,003
Fine tuning raised that to fifty nine point eight

73
00:02:49,003 --> 00:02:52,116
percent. Claude Opus five point five was more accurate

74
00:02:52,116 --> 00:02:54,018
at seventy one point six percent.

75
00:02:54,018 --> 00:02:55,170
On an H one hundred,

76
00:02:55,170 --> 00:02:57,879
my model took about zero point nine seconds per

77
00:02:57,879 --> 00:03:00,185
request versus nine point nine for Opus.

78
00:03:00,185 --> 00:03:03,585
In bulk, classification cost was about one hundred fiftieth

79
00:03:03,585 --> 00:03:06,179
of Opus, excluding idle capacity and hosting.

80
00:03:07,039 --> 00:03:09,576
The hardest part was making the test honest.

81
00:03:09,576 --> 00:03:12,631
My first version always included the right code among

82
00:03:12,631 --> 00:03:15,744
eight choices. I rebuilt it with fresh rulings fetched

83
00:03:15,744 --> 00:03:18,858
individually, removed sentences that revealed answers,

84
00:03:18,858 --> 00:03:21,683
and froze the test rules before scoring any tuned

85
00:03:21,683 --> 00:03:24,623
model. Search now misses the code in twenty percent

86
00:03:24,623 --> 00:03:25,718
of cases. On those,

87
00:03:25,718 --> 00:03:28,197
Opus gets thirty percent right from memory;

88
00:03:28,197 --> 00:03:29,696
my model gets one percent.

89
00:03:29,696 --> 00:03:32,867
Memory drills raised recall without search from zero to

90
00:03:32,867 --> 00:03:35,058
forty six percent on development data,

91
00:03:35,058 --> 00:03:36,787
but barely helped with search.

92
00:03:36,787 --> 00:03:39,209
That follow up was not scored on the final

93
00:03:39,209 --> 00:03:41,861
test; the reported checkpoint stayed the same.

94
00:03:41,861 --> 00:03:45,493
Training on four hundred eighty eight verified explanations did

95
00:03:45,493 --> 00:03:48,836
not help either. Those experiments showed where the limits

96
00:03:48,836 --> 00:03:49,067
are.

97
00:03:49,927 --> 00:03:52,877
I published the model and dataset on Hugging Face,

98
00:03:52,877 --> 00:03:55,296
alongside a live demo and results report.

99
00:03:55,296 --> 00:03:58,009
Fine tuning took my small model from forty two

100
00:03:58,009 --> 00:03:59,366
to about sixty percent.

101
00:03:59,366 --> 00:04:01,018
On the faster test hardware,

102
00:04:01,018 --> 00:04:03,496
it is about eleven times faster than Opus,

103
00:04:03,496 --> 00:04:05,856
but roughly twelve points less accurate.
